 "result_text": "I didn't change any source. The issue is about the test, not the Louvain code, and the test is off-limits for this task.\n\nThe relaxed assertion is correct. Louvain is randomized, so a higher `threshold` stops earlier and can't give a higher modularity than the default. It can give an equal one, and for graphs other than the seed-10 graph in `test_threshold`, it often does. So `assert mod1 < mod2` only holds for that one graph and seed, and `mod1 <= mod2` is the right claim.\n\nI read `louvain_partitions` and `_one_level` in `networkx/algorithms/community/louvain.py` and found nothing wrong with how the threshold is applied. A strict `<` in the test is just too strong.\n\nThe fix is a one-line change in `networkx/algorithms/community/tests/test_louvain.py`, line 239: `assert mod1 < mod2` becomes `assert mod1 <= mod2`. I left it alone because you asked me not to modify tests. I can apply it if you want.\n\nI didn't run the test suite.",
